(i) **Nucleopolyhedrovirus**, a genus of baculoviruses, is used as a narrow-spectrum insecticidal biocontrol agent; baculoviruses are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods.

(ii) Its use helps integrated pest management because:
- it is **species-specific** and has narrow-spectrum insecticidal action;
- it has **no negative impact on plants, mammals, birds, fish or even non-target insects**, so beneficial insects are conserved;
- this makes it especially desirable when an ecologically sensitive area is being treated, and it reduces dependence on toxic chemical pesticides.

A malignant tumour is more damaging because:
- its cells **grow very rapidly**, invading and damaging the surrounding normal tissues;
- the actively dividing cells **starve the normal cells** by competing with them for vital nutrients;
- it shows **metastasis**: cells slough off from the tumour, reach distant sites through the blood, and start new tumours wherever they lodge.

A benign tumour, in contrast, normally remains confined to its original location, does not spread to other parts of the body and causes little damage.

