The Bt toxin does not kill the bacterium that makes it because Bacillus thuringiensis produces it in an **inactive form, as a protoxin** (insecticidal crystal protein). It becomes harmful only inside the insect:
- the insect pest **ingests** the inactive toxin;
- the **alkaline pH of the insect gut** solubilises the crystals and converts the protoxin into the **active toxin**;
- the activated toxin **binds to the surface of the midgut epithelial cells**;
- it **creates pores** in these cells;
- the cells **swell and lyse**;
- this eventually causes the **death of the insect**.

The antibody is drawn as a **Y-shaped** molecule made of four polypeptide chains (\(\text{H}_2\text{L}_2\)):
- two long **heavy chains**, which together form the stem of the Y and continue into its two arms;
- two short **light chains**, one lying alongside the outer part of each arm;
- **disulphide bonds** joining each light chain to its heavy chain and the two heavy chains to each other;
- an **antigen binding site** at the tip of each arm, formed by the ends of a heavy and a light chain.

Labels: heavy chain, light chain, disulphide bond, antigen binding site (any four).

The sperm (22 + Y) and the ovum (22 + XX) together give a zygote with **44 + XXY**, that is 47 chromosomes. The offspring suffers from **Klinefelter's syndrome**, caused by the presence of an additional copy of the X chromosome. Any two symptoms:
- overall masculine development, but also feminine development such as development of breasts (**gynaecomastia**);
- the individual is **sterile**;
- tall stature.

The best known and most common autosomal aneuploidy is **Down's syndrome**, caused by an additional copy of chromosome number 21 (trisomy of 21). Any two symptoms:
- short stature with a small round head;
- furrowed tongue and a partially open mouth;
- broad palm with a characteristic palm crease;
- retarded physical, psychomotor and mental development.

(i) After implantation, finger-like projections called **chorionic villi** appear on the **trophoblast**. These villi are surrounded by the uterine tissue and maternal blood. The chorionic villi and the uterine tissue become **interdigitated** with each other and jointly form a structural and functional unit between the developing embryo (foetus) and the maternal body, called the **placenta**. The placenta is connected to the embryo through the **umbilical cord**; it supplies oxygen and nutrients to the embryo and removes carbon dioxide and excretory wastes, and it also acts as an endocrine tissue producing hormones such as hCG, hPL, estrogens and progestogens.

(ii) The diagram shows the uterus in section with the foetus inside it:
- the **embryo** lying in the fluid-filled amniotic cavity within the **cavity of the uterus**;
- the **umbilical cord with its vessels** running from the embryo to the placenta;
- the **placental villi** embedded in the uterine wall;
- the **yolk sac** beside the embryo;
- the **plug of mucus in the cervix** at the lower end of the uterus.

Labels (any four): placental villi, umbilical cord with its vessels, yolk sac, embryo, cavity of uterus, plug of mucus in cervix.

(i) A bioreactor must provide the **optimal growth conditions** to achieve the desired product. Any four of: **temperature, pH, substrate, salts, vitamins and oxygen**. (A stirred-tank bioreactor maintains these with an agitator system, an oxygen delivery system, a foam control system, a temperature control system, a pH control system and sampling ports.)

(ii) The statement refers to the **log (exponential) phase** of growth, the physiologically most active phase of the cells, in which they grow and divide fastest; in continuous culture the cells are maintained in this phase to obtain a larger biomass and higher yields.

(iii) **No.** The product formed in the bioreactor has to go through **downstream processing** before it is ready for marketing as a finished product:
- **separation and purification** of the product;
- **formulation with suitable preservatives**;
- strict **quality control testing**, and in the case of drugs, clinical trials.
